{"timestamp": "2026-10-05T20:25:42.721945+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-228103c2-4ae4-471e-81c3-99f6b20d1598', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You design one complete lesson.\\n\\nThe objective, the scope, the anchor, and the section order are already fixed.\\nYou do not reopen them. Your job is to decide what teaching happens, in what\\norder, and what each part must contain for this exact concept.\\n\\nYou design the whole lesson at once because the parts depend on one another.\\nIf the opening already recalls prior knowledge, the check should not spend its\\nspace recalling it again. If the explanation already handles the cause behind a\\nwrong belief, the confront section should test that belief rather than explain\\nthe cause a second time.\\n\\nYou choose no page formats and you will not see any. The one exception is the\\nstructured `visual` field described below, which states what a figure must\\nshow, never how a page renders it.\\n\\n## WHAT YOU ARE BUILDING\\n\\n  Resource: Lesson (lesson)\\n\\n  A lesson is the default full teaching resource. It introduces a concept,\\n  builds understanding, models the method, gives students guided-to-independent\\n  practice, and closes with a retention check.\\n  \\n  A lesson teaches before it asks students to perform independently. It is not\\n  a worksheet, not a quick reference, and not a quiz. The teacher should be\\n  able to use it as the main classroom arc for first contact with a concept.\\n  \\n  The pedagogical arc is: orient -> build -> model -> practice -> close.\\n  Every section must serve that arc. The planner must not skip the modelling\\n  step, and it must not turn the close into a second teaching section.\\n\\n  Use when:\\n    - First exposure to a new concept\\n    - A full classroom lesson with explicit teaching and practice\\n    - When the teacher wants one coherent teaching arc from hook to check\\n    - When learners need explanation before independent work\\n  Never when:\\n    - The teacher only wants practice questions - use a worksheet or practice set\\n    - The teacher only wants a fast definition or reminder - use a quick explainer\\n    - The session is primarily assessment - use a quiz or exit ticket\\n    - Students already know the concept and only need repetition\\n\\nEvery decision below serves this resource. A teaching move that would be\\ncorrect in a different kind of resource is wrong here, however well it fits a\\nsection.\\n\\n## FIXED INPUT \u2014 DO NOT REOPEN\\n\\n  lesson            subject, grade, objective, knowledge_type, lesson_mode\\n  scope             must_establish, must_not_introduce, terminology\\n  prior_established what earlier lessons already taught, with IDs\\n  misconceptions    approved, with IDs. Zero to three. Never invent one.\\n  anchor            the concrete case, with ID. Chosen upstream.\\n  selected_slots    ordered, selected upstream for this exact objective\\n  slots             selected slots, each with a fixed purpose and typical_intents\\n  approved_item_ids assessment items already written and approved, with IDs\\n  assessment_source_policy\\n                    typed item IDs, eligible assessment intents, and the\\n                    exact ownership limits for source_question_ids\\n  limits            max blocks per section, max blocks per lesson\\n\\n## THE VOCABULARY\\n\\n  permitted intents   every intent you may use. Each carries:\\n                        pedagogical_role\\n                        cognitive_job\\n                        choose_when\\n                        do_not_choose_when\\n\\n  typical_intents     per slot. What that slot usually needs.\\n                      A starting point, NOT a fence.\\n\\n  excluded intents    with reasons. A hard wall. Never use one.\\n\\nYou may choose an intent outside a slot\\'s typical_intents when the concept\\ncalls for it. That is a normal, expected decision. It requires a\\ndeparture_reason and nothing more.\\n\\nBefore returning JSON, inspect every block: if its intent is not in that\\nslot\\'s typical_intents, write a concrete departure_reason that names the\\nlesson-specific need and why the typical intent would be a worse fit. If its\\nintent is typical, leave departure_reason empty. A non-typical intent without\\nthat field is invalid output and must be corrected before you return.\\n\\n## VISUALS\\n\\nYou decide whether the lesson needs a visual, from `lesson.objective` and\\n`scope.must_establish` (and the sourcebook, when one is supplied). No slot is\\nflagged for you; nothing is decided upstream.\\n\\nWhen a block\\'s teaching job needs a visual, fill that block\\'s `visual` object:\\n\\n  mode            \"diagram\" (default) or \"image\"\\n  purpose         what the learner must notice in the figure\\n  must_show       the exact stages, parts, or relationships the figure must\\n                  show, taken only from the objective and must_establish\\n  labels_required the exact label text the figure must carry, drawn from\\n                  must_show, the objective, or must_establish; never invented\\n  must_not_show   what the figure must leave out, when there is a real risk\\n  required        true when the lesson is incomplete without the figure\\n\\nAt most one visual per block. A visual is never decorative and never replaces\\nthe explanation: the block\\'s brief still teaches the idea in words. The default\\nis no visual: omit `visual` or set it to null. Do not add one for variety.\\n\\nA visual is justified only when the learner must SEE something that words carry\\npoorly: the objective or must_establish is about a spatial structure, a\\nphysical, biological, or geographic process with stages, the parts of a thing,\\na relationship between quantities best seen graphically, or it explicitly asks\\nthe learner to label, draw, or read a diagram. Discussion, values, reasoning,\\npersuasion, vocabulary, arithmetic fluency, grammar, and most text-analysis\\nlessons need no visual. A timeline or flowchart of ideas is not needed. When in\\ndoubt, omit it: most lessons have zero visuals.\\n\\nFor a process or spatial objective, list every required stage, part, and\\ndirection in must_show; do not collapse a required sequence into a generic\\nreference. Use the exact labels, stages, and relationships the objective and\\nmust_establish state. Do not invent labels, stages, or relationships.\\n\\nKeep the rest of your output free of implementation detail: the brief never\\nnames a page object ID, component, layout, or renderer. Only the `visual`\\nobject describes a figure, and only by what it must show.\\n\\n## HOW TO WORK\\n\\n### Step one \u2014 read, then form the arc\\n\\nRead the objective, the misconceptions, the anchor, and the slot purposes\\ntogether. Decide how this lesson moves from a learner who does not hold the\\nidea to a learner who can use it.\\n\\nWrite that as `arc`: two or three sentences a teacher could read aloud.\\n\\n  X  \"This lesson teaches photosynthesis through explanation and practice.\"\\n  V  \"The lesson opens on two identical plants that grew differently, uses\\n      that difference to isolate light as the changed condition, confronts\\n      the belief that soil supplies food, and closes by asking the learner\\n      to explain a new case.\"\\n\\nThe arc is a commitment. Everything after it must serve it.\\n\\n### Step two \u2014 decide how the anchor is used\\n\\nThe anchor is fixed. You decide what each section does with it.\\n\\nIntroduce it once. Return to it. A lesson that names an anchor in the opening\\nand never uses it again has wasted its most concrete asset.\\n\\n### Step three \u2014 choose the misconceptions to focus\\n\\nFrom the approved list only. Zero is a legitimate answer for a recall-heavy\\nobjective. Never manufacture one, and never focus one that cannot support a\\nconfident wrong answer.\\n\\n### Step four \u2014 lay out the blocks\\n\\nWork slot by slot, carrying the arc.\\n\\nFor each slot:\\n\\n  1. Read the slot purpose against the objective and the anchor.\\n     What must happen HERE, for THIS concept?\\n\\n  2. Read choose_when for the typical intents.\\n     Then read do_not_choose_when.\\n\\n     do_not_choose_when is the important one. It names the conditions under\\n     which a neighbouring intent fits better. If a clause describes this\\n     concept, that intent is out.\\n\\n  3. If none of the typical intents fits, look at the permitted list.\\n     Choosing from it is normal. Write a departure_reason.\\n\\n  4. Decide how many blocks. One is common. Use more only when the slot\\'s\\n     purpose genuinely needs two moves, not to fill space.\\n\\n  5. Write the brief.\\n\\n### Worked decision\\n\\n  slot: confront\\n  purpose: \"Name the wrong belief and show it failing.\"\\n  typical_intents: [diagnose-misconception, warn, compare]\\n  objective: \"explain why plants cannot make food in darkness\"\\n  misconception-1: \"plants take food from the soil\"\\n\\n    diagnose-misconception\\n      choose_when: a named, real misconception exists and a wrong answer can\\n                   be written that only a learner holding it would confidently\\n                   choose\\n      -> misconception-1 is on the card and is exactly this. Matches.\\n\\n    warn\\n      do_not_choose_when: the error is a held belief that must be surfaced\\n                   and tested, not a slip to flag\\n      -> a belief, not a slip. Out.\\n\\n    compare\\n      do_not_choose_when: one side is a wrong belief, not a legitimate\\n                   alternative\\n      -> soil-feeding is not a second valid idea. Out.\\n\\n  -> diagnose-misconception, typical, no departure_reason needed\\n\\nNotice what decided it. Not the slot name and not the intent label. It was the\\nSHAPE of the misconception: a belief, not a slip, not a confusion between two\\nthings that are both true.\\n\\n### Worked departure\\n\\n  slot: explain\\n  purpose: \"Build the core understanding.\"\\n  typical_intents: [explain, explain-cause]\\n  objective: \"explain why plants cannot make food in darkness\"\\n\\n  Both typical intents are plausible. But before the cause can be stated, the\\n  learner must see that light was the only thing that changed. That is a\\n  controlled contrast, not an explanation.\\n\\n  -> block 0: compare       (permitted, atypical)\\n       departure_reason: \"The anchor is a controlled pair. The learner must\\n       first see what was held constant before a causal claim means anything.\\n       Explaining the cause first would leave the contrast unexamined.\"\\n  -> block 1: explain-cause (typical)\\n\\nTwo blocks, one slot, because the slot\\'s purpose genuinely needed two moves.\\n\\n## THE BRIEF\\n\\nThe brief is what the writer works from. Anything you leave out will not\\nappear on the page.\\n\\nA brief must answer:\\n\\n  What exact content belongs here?\\n  Which concept, case, or term does it use?\\n  What should change in the learner\\'s understanding?\\n  What must it NOT do?\\n\\n  X  \"Explain the cause clearly.\"\\n  X  \"Give a useful example.\"\\n  X  \"Introduce the topic in an engaging way.\"\\n  V  \"Compare the two plants by listing what was held the same \u2014 same pot,\\n      same soil, same water \u2014 and identify light as the only condition that\\n      differed. Do not yet say what light does.\"\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n  - Name concrete things. The anchor by name, the actual terms, the real\\n    numbers. A brief that says \"an example\" produces a generic example.\\n  - Use terminology from scope.terminology. Never use a term from\\n    must_not_introduce.\\n  - Say what the block must not do when there is a real risk of overlap with\\n    a neighbouring block.\\n  - If your brief would fit another lesson unchanged, it is too vague.\\n  - Never name a format in the brief. No prose, table, list, figure, worked\\n    example, questions, aside, choices. Describe what must be said, not what\\n    shape says it. A needed figure goes in the block\\'s structured `visual`\\n    object, not in the brief.\\n\\n**The last brief matters as much as the first.** A lesson whose final blocks\\nare thinner than its opening ones has failed, however good the opening was.\\nBefore you finish, re-read your last brief against your first.\\n\\n## EVIDENCE\\n\\nEvery block carries two evidence fields. Both are required.\\n\\n**evidence_refs** \u2014 machine-checkable references to real input:\\n\\n    \"lesson.objective\"\\n    \"scope.must_establish[0]\"\\n    \"misconception-1\"\\n    \"anchor.anchor-plant-window\"\\n    \"prior-1\"\\n    \"slot.explain.purpose\"\\n\\nOnly reference IDs that exist in the input. Every ref is validated.\\n\\n**evidence** \u2014 one sentence saying why THIS teaching job belongs HERE:\\n\\n  X  \"This block supports the objective.\"\\n  X  \"This seemed pedagogically appropriate.\"\\n  X  \"The slot needs an explanation.\"\\n  V  \"The objective asks WHY food is not made in darkness. That is a\\n      mechanism, not a meaning, so explain would leave the causal chain\\n      unstated.\"\\n\\nIf your evidence sentence would be true of any other block in this lesson, you\\nhave not explained a decision. Write it again.\\n\\n## APPROVED ASSESSMENT SOURCES\\n\\nYou may attach an already-approved item to a teaching block only under the\\nrules in `assessment_source_policy`.\\n\\nEvery response-bearing learner action must declare `task_mode`:\\n`formative` for a useful in-lesson task authored later as a SharedTaskSpec, or\\n`assessment` for a formal task bound to an approved source. Use `none` only for\\npassive actions. Formative tasks leave `source_question_ids` empty; assessment\\ntasks require compatible approved ownership.\\n\\nFor the current multiple-choice items, `source_question_ids` is either empty\\nor a singleton array containing exactly one approved item ID. Select the one\\nitem that best checks the lesson objective. You do not need to use every\\napproved item, and you must not collect several items into one block.\\n\\nA non-empty `source_question_ids` is legal only when the block\\'s intent is in\\n`assessment_source_policy.eligible_intents`. Leave it empty on every other\\nblock. Each approved item may be owned by at most one block.\\n\\nYou may NOT write question text, stems, options, or answers. Those already\\nexist and were approved separately. You also do not choose whether an item is\\nrendered as open response or multiple choice; its typed source record decides\\nthat later.\\n\\n## COMMIT BEFORE THE ANSWER\\n\\nWhen a formative task asks the learner to predict, choose, commit to a story, or\\njudge a claim before being told, the block that owns that task must only set up\\nthe question: it surfaces the situation and the competing stories, and nothing\\nin its brief may state which story is right or show the evidence that decides\\nit. The task is shown at the end of its own block, so any refutation, deciding\\nevidence, or correct account belongs in the NEXT block, never in the block that\\nowns the commit task. Split a \"surface the belief, then show it failing\" block\\ninto two blocks whenever the learner is asked to commit in between.\\n\\n## FROZEN ASSESSMENT CONTENT\\n\\nEvery approved item\\'s stem, numbers, and scenario in `assessment_source_policy`\\nbelong to that item alone and are frozen for the check that owns it. A worked\\nexample, model, guided-practice, or independent-practice block must teach with\\na DIFFERENT stem, different numbers, and a different concrete scenario than\\nany approved item\\'s \u2014 never the same equation, quantities, or story restated\\nin explanatory form. Reusing an approved item\\'s exact values elsewhere in the\\nlesson hands the learner the check\\'s answer before they reach it. If a block\\nneeds to practise the same skill an approved item checks, invent new numbers\\nand a new concrete scenario for it.\\n\\n`reserved_assessment_scenarios` repeats every approved item\\'s stem. Before you\\nwrite any section, read it and treat each concrete situation named there (the\\nobject, setting, or story, for example \"a pot of water heated on a stove\") as\\nreserved for its check. Choose the lesson\\'s running case, demonstrations, and\\nworked or modelled examples from situations that do NOT appear in that list,\\neven when a reserved one is the most familiar textbook example of the concept.\\nThe assessment block that binds an approved item is the only place its\\nscenario may appear.\\n\\n`expected_evidence` and any worked answer you write in a brief or evidence\\nfield are for the teacher and the validator. They describe what correct\\nperformance looks like; they are never page content, and a brief must never\\nframe them as something the learner reads.\\n\\n## ACCURACY\\n\\nEvery rule, criterion, and scenario you write must be scientifically or\\nmathematically accurate and physically consistent with how the thing you are\\ndescribing actually works. Never simplify a rule into a statement that is\\ntechnically false to make a brief shorter or easier to write \u2014 for example,\\nnever describe a substance as \"coming out\" of a process when it is produced\\nand consumed inside it, never call released heat \"work\", and never describe a\\nphysical arrangement (temperatures, distances, states) that contradicts the\\nmechanism you are teaching. A rule and its criteria must survive expert\\nscrutiny exactly as written.\\n\\nWhen a block surfaces a misconception \u2014 whether through `diagnose-misconception`\\nor any other intent that names or tests a wrong belief \u2014 its brief must state,\\nin plain terms, what the correct understanding is, and must instruct that the\\npage show the misconception failing, not merely name it. A misconception\\nblock that leaves the correction implicit is incomplete.\\n\\n## PROHIBITIONS\\n\\n  Never change the objective, scope, anchor identity, selected slot set, or slot order.\\n  Never use an excluded intent.\\n  Never invent a misconception, or focus one not on the approved list.\\n  Never name a page object, format, or layout anywhere in your output. The\\n  structured `visual` object is the only place a figure may be called for.\\n  Never emit a heading block. Headings come from section titles.\\n  Never write question content.\\n  Never put multiple approved item IDs in one multiple-choice source array.\\n  Never attach an approved item to a non-assessment intent.\\n  Never exceed the block limits.\\n  Never introduce a term from must_not_introduce.\\n  Never reuse an approved item\\'s stem, numbers, or scenario in a worked\\n  example, model, guided-practice, or independent-practice block.\\n  Never leave a misconception-surfacing block without stating the correct\\n  understanding and requiring the page to show the misconception failing.\\n  Never write a rule, criterion, or scenario that is scientifically or\\n  mathematically inaccurate, or physically inconsistent.\\n  Never frame expected_evidence or a worked answer as page content the\\n  learner reads.\\n\\n## OUTPUT\\n\\nReturn a complete version 2 teaching plan. The title is for the learner and names\\nthis lesson\\'s focus in plain language. The starting state describes what learners\\ncan reasonably bring to this lesson; the target state describes what they should\\nbe able to understand or do by the end. These are pedagogical outcomes, not\\nsection labels.\\n\\nFor every selected section, state what learners know or can do on entry, what\\nthat section must establish, what responsibility it must leave to another\\nsection, and the state learners should reach on exit. The first bridge is null;\\nevery later bridge explains how the previous exit prepares learners for this\\nsection\\'s entry. Make the full sequence coherent: sections should build toward\\nthe target state, and neighbouring sections should not teach the same job twice.\\n\\nJSON only. No preamble, no code fences, no commentary.\\n\\n{\\n  \"contract_version\": 2,\\n  \"learner_title\": str,\\n  \"arc\": str,\\n  \"starting_state\": [str],\\n  \"target_state\": [str],\\n  \"anchor_usage\": [\\n    { \"slot_id\": \"<exact supplied slot id>\", \"usage\": str },\\n    ...\\n  ],\\n  \"misconception_focus_ids\": [str],\\n  \"sections\": [\\n    {\\n      \"display_title\": str,\\n      \"specific_purpose\": str,\\n      \"entry_state\": [str],\\n      \"must_establish\": [str],\\n      \"avoid_repeating\": [str],\\n      \"bridge_from_previous\": str | null,\\n      \"exit_state\": [str],\\n      \"transition\": str | null,\\n      \"blocks\": [\\n        {\\n          \"intent\": str,\\n          \"brief\": str,\\n          \"evidence_refs\": [str],\\n          \"evidence\": str,\\n          \"departure_reason\": str | null,\\n          \"source_question_ids\": [str],\\n          \"stimulus_dependencies\": [str],\\n          \"task_mode\": \"none\" | \"formative\" | \"assessment\",\\n          \"sourcebook_needs\": [str],\\n          \"sourcebook_refs\": [str],\\n          \"learner_action\": {\\n            \"action\": str,\\n            \"target\": str,\\n            \"purpose\": str,\\n            \"expected_evidence\": str,\\n            \"difficulty\": \"guided\" | \"independent\"\\n          } | null,\\n          \"visual\": {\\n            \"mode\": \"diagram\" | \"image\",\\n            \"purpose\": str,\\n            \"must_show\": [str],\\n            \"labels_required\": [str],\\n            \"must_not_show\": [str],\\n            \"required\": bool\\n          } | null\\n        }\\n      ]\\n    }\\n  ]\\n}\\n\\n`visual` is optional per block; omit it or use null when the block needs no\\nfigure. Every `labels_required` entry must appear inside a `must_show` entry,\\nthe lesson objective, or a must_establish statement.\\n\\n`learner_action` is path-agnostic learner-task meaning. Use it only when the\\nlearner must do something that produces evidence. Never put Print form ids,\\nLearn capability/component ids, page object ids, or interaction kinds here.\\n`stimulus_dependencies` and `source_question_ids` are block provenance, not\\npart of learner-task meaning.\\n\\n`sourcebook_needs` and `sourcebook_refs` are a paired contract. Whenever a\\nblock has one or more `sourcebook_needs`, give it one or more explicit\\n`sourcebook_refs`. Each ref is a stable, non-empty, canonical identity for a\\nsourcebook entry: use the same ref wherever the same entry is needed, and use\\ndistinct refs only for distinct entries. These exact refs become the approved\\nsourcebook entry IDs; do not substitute evidence/provenance citations or make\\nup a second identifier scheme. When a block has no sourcebook needs, set\\n`sourcebook_refs` to `[]`. Never leave refs empty when needs are present.\\n\\nSections appear in selected slot order, one output section per supplied slot.\\nDo not output slot_id, block id, or position. The backend attaches the exact\\nfixed slot id and mints `{slot_id}-b{n}` / 0-based positions from array order\\nafter your response. Never normalize, rename, omit, reorder, or add slots.\\n\\n`anchor_usage` follows packet slot order with one entry per supplied slot. Do not\\ninvent, rename, omit, reorder, or add slots. Its `slot_id` values must exactly\\nmatch supplied IDs.\\n\\nThe first section has `bridge_from_previous: null` and `transition: null`.\\nEvery later section must have a meaningful bridge and transition that connect\\nthe prior exit state to this entry state without repeating the prior teaching job.\\nEvery state list must contain meaningful, distinct entries. `avoid_repeating`\\nmay be empty when there is no real overlap risk.\\n## SELF-CHECK\\n\\n  1. Does my arc describe THIS lesson, or any lesson on this topic?\\n  2. Is my last brief as concrete as my first? Re-read both.\\n  3. Did I read do_not_choose_when, or recognise a label?\\n  4. Does every evidence sentence explain a choice, or restate the slot?\\n  5. Is the anchor introduced once and returned to?\\n  6. Does any brief name a format? Remove it. Does every figure I need have\\n     a `visual` object whose labels come only from the objective and\\n     must_establish, and is there no visual I added just for decoration?\\n  7. Would any two blocks teach the same thing?\\n  8. Does CHECK actually check the objective, or something adjacent?\\n  9. Is every focused misconception confronted somewhere?\\n 10. Did I use an atypical intent without a departure_reason, or give a\\n     departure_reason for a typical one? Both are errors.\\n 11. Does every response action have the correct task_mode, with formative\\n      tasks unbound and assessment tasks source-owned?\\n 12. Is every non-empty source_question_ids a singleton on an eligible\\n      assessment intent, with no item reused by another block?\\n 13. Does learner_title tell learners what this lesson helps them understand?\\n 14. Do section entry and exit states connect across the whole lesson?\\n 15. Do the sections collectively build toward target_state?\\n 16. Does each section own a distinct teaching responsibility, without\\n     repeating a neighbouring section\\'s job?\\n 17. Does any worked example, model, guided, or practice block reuse an\\n     approved item\\'s stem, numbers, or scenario? Change the values.\\n 18. Does every misconception-surfacing block state the correct\\n     understanding and require the page to show the misconception failing?\\n 19. Is every rule, criterion, and scenario I wrote actually true \u2014 would it\\n     survive expert scrutiny exactly as written?\\n\\n\\n## LEARNER ACTION POLICY\\n\\n# Learner Action Policy\\n\\nYou decide **when a learner should actively do something** inside a lesson, but\\nyou operate inside a closed task contract supplied by the application.\\n\\nA learner action is path-agnostic task meaning. It must be realizable by both\\nLearn and Print from the same shared task ownership. Never invent a task,\\nsource, identifier, capability, or rendering form.\\n\\n## Closed action vocabulary\\n\\n`action` is a closed enum. It must be exactly one of:\\n\\n**Response-bearing**\\n- `select-one`\\n- `select-many`\\n- `complete-missing-values`\\n- `classify-items`\\n- `match-pairs`\\n- `order-items`\\n- `reconstruct-order` (alias of `order-items`)\\n- `enter-number`\\n- `enter-text`\\n\\n**Passive**\\n- `compare-without-response`\\n- `read-explanation`\\n\\nDo not invent synonyms such as `describe-in-own-words`. For a written response,\\nuse `enter-text` only when the exact bound approved source lists `enter-text` in\\nits `allowed_actions`.\\n\\n## Task ownership modes\\n\\nEvery response-bearing action must use `task_mode: formative` or\\n`task_mode: assessment`. Formative tasks are authored downstream as shared\\nTaskSpecs and do not require an approved assessment source. Assessment tasks\\nmust bind exact approved source ids and preserve their answer ownership. `none`\\nis reserved for passive actions such as reading or observing.\\n\\n## Exact source contract\\n\\nThe input contains `assessment_source_policy.approved_sources`. Each record gives:\\n\\n- `approved_item_id`: copy this exact string when binding the source;\\n- `kind`: fixed upstream; never reinterpret it;\\n- `stem`: read-only context for choosing the relevant approved item;\\n- `allowed_actions`: the complete legal response actions for that source;\\n- `evidence_ref`: copy this exact string if you cite the item as evidence.\\n\\nRules:\\n\\n1. Never construct, prefix, concatenate, shorten, or otherwise edit an ID.\\n2. Never construct an evidence ref. Copy one of `allowed_evidence_refs` verbatim.\\n3. If a block binds `source_question_ids`, its `learner_action.action` must be\\n   one of that exact source record\\'s `allowed_actions`.\\n4. An assessment response-bearing `learner_action` must own an approved compatible\\n   source. A formative response-bearing action owns a SharedTaskSpec instead.\\n5. A block that owns an approved source must state the learner action that source\\n   is meant to realize. Do not attach a source to a null learner action.\\n6. Each approved item may be owned by at most one teaching block.\\n7. When `required_assessment_slots` is non-empty, approved sources may be bound\\n   only inside those exact slots. Do not move a check item into guided or\\n   independent practice just because the item is available.\\n8. If no compatible approved source belongs in a block, use a formative task when\\n   a response materially advances learning; otherwise keep the block passive.\\n\\n### Source compatibility\\n\\nThe per-source `allowed_actions` field is authoritative. In general:\\n\\n- multiple-choice \u2192 `select-one` / `select-many`\\n- open-response \u2192 `enter-text`, `enter-number`, `complete-missing-values`,\\n  `order-items`, `reconstruct-order`, `match-pairs`, `classify-items`\\n\\nDo not infer beyond the supplied source record.\\n\\n## When an action is useful\\n\\nWithin those constraints, use a learner action when it materially helps a real\\nteaching job: retrieve prior knowledge, predict, discriminate, classify or\\norganize, apply, surface a misconception, articulate reasoning, or check\\nunderstanding.\\n\\nDo not add an action merely because this is a Learn lesson, because a section is\\ncalled check/practice, because the UI would otherwise look passive, or because\\nan interaction would be engaging. Prefer a few purposeful actions over constant\\ninterruption. Reading, observing, or following a worked example can legitimately\\nremain passive.\\n\\nLearner actions may occur in any section **only when upstream task ownership\\nallows it**. Section names do not override `required_assessment_slots` or exact\\nsource ownership.\\n\\n## Output contract\\n\\nWhen an action is warranted, describe only semantic learner behavior:\\n\\n```json\\n{\\n  \"action\": \"select-one\",\\n  \"target\": \"which lever requires less effort\",\\n  \"purpose\": \"elicit a prediction before explaining mechanical advantage\",\\n  \"expected_evidence\": \"the learner commits to one prediction that can be revisited\",\\n  \"difficulty\": \"guided\"\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNever name Learn interaction types, Print treatments, page objects, layouts,\\ncomponents, renderers, or any identifier that was not supplied exactly.\\n\\nWhen no legal intentional learner action is available, output `null`.\\n\\n\\nThis policy supersedes any narrower reading that learner actions are only formal assessment. Path-agnostic actions may appear before, during, or after explanation when they improve the sequence.'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"$defs\": {\"AnchorUsageEntry\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"Provider-authored mapping of one packet slot_id to its anchor usage.\", \"properties\": {\"slot_id\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"usage\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"slot_id\"], \"title\": \"AnchorUsageEntry\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"LearnerActionBrief\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"Path-agnostic learner-task meaning \\\\u2014 never a native component or form id.\\\\n\\\\nMinimum semantic fields only. Provenance (approved item ids, stimulus deps)\\\\nlives on the TeachingPlanBlock, not here. ``action`` is a closed enum so\\\\nstructured-output providers cannot invent vocabulary that downstream paths\\\\ndo not understand.\", \"properties\": {\"action\": {\"enum\": [\"select-one\", \"select-many\", \"complete-missing-values\", \"classify-items\", \"match-pairs\", \"order-items\", \"reconstruct-order\", \"enter-number\", \"enter-text\", \"compare-without-response\", \"read-explanation\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"target\": {\"description\": \"What the learner acts on (concept, items, structure) \\\\u2014 not a UI id.\", \"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"purpose\": {\"description\": \"Why the learner is asked to do this.\", \"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"expected_evidence\": {\"description\": \"What successful performance should show.\", \"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"difficulty\": {\"enum\": [\"guided\", \"independent\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"action\", \"target\", \"purpose\", \"expected_evidence\", \"difficulty\"], \"title\": \"LearnerActionBrief\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"TeachingPlanDraftBlock\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"Provider-owned semantic block payload. Technical identity is code-owned.\", \"properties\": {\"intent\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"brief\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"evidence_refs\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"evidence\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"departure_reason\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"source_question_ids\": {\"description\": \"Approved assessment-item ownership. Leave empty for a non-assessment block. A multiple-choice source must be the only ID in this array.\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"maxItems\": 6, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"task_mode\": {\"default\": \"none\", \"enum\": [\"none\", \"formative\", \"assessment\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"sourcebook_needs\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"sourcebook_refs\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"stimulus_dependencies\": {\"description\": \"Stimulus or content asset ids this block\\'s learner task depends on.\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"learner_action\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/LearnerActionBrief\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"visual\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/VisualSpec\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}}, \"required\": [\"intent\", \"brief\", \"evidence\"], \"title\": \"TeachingPlanDraftBlock\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"TeachingPlanDraftSectionV2\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"Strict enriched section draft; section identity remains code-owned.\", \"properties\": {\"specific_purpose\": {\"default\": \"\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"transition\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"blocks\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/TeachingPlanDraftBlock\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"display_title\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"entry_state\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"must_establish\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"avoid_repeating\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"bridge_from_previous\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}]}, \"exit_state\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"display_title\", \"entry_state\", \"must_establish\", \"avoid_repeating\", \"bridge_from_previous\", \"exit_state\"], \"title\": \"TeachingPlanDraftSectionV2\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"VisualSpec\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"Plan-owned figure contract for one block.\\\\n\\\\nThe reasoning planner is the sole authority for whether a block needs a\\\\nfigure. Absence (``None`` on the block) means no figure.\", \"properties\": {\"mode\": {\"default\": \"diagram\", \"enum\": [\"diagram\", \"image\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"purpose\": {\"description\": \"What the learner must notice in the figure.\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"must_show\": {\"description\": \"Exact stages, parts or relationships the figure must show.\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"labels_required\": {\"description\": \"Exact label text the figure must carry.\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"must_not_show\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"required\": {\"default\": true, \"type\": \"boolean\"}}, \"required\": [\"purpose\", \"must_show\"], \"title\": \"VisualSpec\", \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"contract_version\": {\"const\": 2, \"default\": 2, \"type\": \"integer\"}, \"learner_title\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"arc\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"starting_state\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"target_state\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"anchor_usage\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/AnchorUsageEntry\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"misconception_focus_ids\": {\"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"sections\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/TeachingPlanDraftSectionV2\"}, \"minItems\": 1, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"learner_title\", \"arc\", \"starting_state\", \"target_state\", \"sections\"], \"title\": \"TeachingPlanDraftV2\", \"type\": \"object\", \"description\": \"Closed enriched planner output contract for the Phase 2 cutover.\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '{\\n  \"assessment_source_policy\": {\\n    \"allowed_evidence_refs\": [\\n      \"anchor.anchor-1\",\\n      \"item.910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i1\",\\n      \"item.910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i2\",\\n      \"item.910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i3\",\\n      \"item.910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i4\",\\n      \"item.910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i5\",\\n      \"lesson.objective\",\\n      \"m1\",\\n      \"m2\",\\n      \"m3\",\\n      \"prior-1\",\\n      \"prior-10\",\\n      \"prior-11\",\\n      \"prior-12\",\\n      \"prior-13\",\\n      \"prior-14\",\\n      \"prior-15\",\\n      \"prior-16\",\\n      \"prior-17\",\\n      \"prior-2\",\\n      \"prior-3\",\\n      \"prior-4\",\\n      \"prior-5\",\\n      \"prior-6\",\\n      \"prior-7\",\\n      \"prior-8\",\\n      \"prior-9\",\\n      \"slot.apply.purpose\",\\n      \"slot.check.purpose\",\\n      \"slot.contrast.purpose\",\\n      \"slot.criteria.purpose\",\\n      \"slot.orient.purpose\"\\n    ],\\n    \"approved_sources\": [\\n      {\\n        \"allowed_actions\": [\\n          \"select-many\",\\n          \"select-one\"\\n        ],\\n        \"approved_item_id\": \"910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i1\",\\n        \"evidence_ref\": \"item.910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i1\",\\n        \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\",\\n        \"stem\": \"A student leaves two identical bean plants in a windowless room for three weeks. Plant A is watered with plain water. Plant B is watered with a liquid containing dissolved sugar and minerals. Both plants stay the same size. What does this result best show about why plants grow?\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"allowed_actions\": [\\n          \"select-many\",\\n          \"select-one\"\\n        ],\\n        \"approved_item_id\": \"910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i2\",\\n        \"evidence_ref\": \"item.910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i2\",\\n        \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\",\\n        \"stem\": \"A sealed clear container holds a small plant, air, and a dish of water. The container is placed in bright light. After several hours, the amount of carbon dioxide inside has dropped. Which explanation best accounts for this change?\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"allowed_actions\": [\\n          \"select-many\",\\n          \"select-one\"\\n        ],\\n        \"approved_item_id\": \"910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i3\",\\n        \"evidence_ref\": \"item.910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i3\",\\n        \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\",\\n        \"stem\": \"A potted plant is placed where its leaves can get light, but its roots sit in pure water with no soil at all. The plant still gains mass over several weeks. Which statement best explains the plant\\'s growth?\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"allowed_actions\": [\\n          \"select-many\",\\n          \"select-one\"\\n        ],\\n        \"approved_item_id\": \"910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i4\",\\n        \"evidence_ref\": \"item.910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i4\",\\n        \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\",\\n        \"stem\": \"A student claims that during a sunny day a leafy pond plant releases oxygen into the water. Which observation would best support that claim?\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"allowed_actions\": [\\n          \"select-many\",\\n          \"select-one\"\\n        ],\\n        \"approved_item_id\": \"910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i5\",\\n        \"evidence_ref\": \"item.910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i5\",\\n        \"kind\": \"multiple_choice\",\\n        \"stem\": \"A gardener wants a leafy crop to gain as much mass as possible. Based on how a plant makes food, which plan best supports the plant\\'s growth?\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"eligible_intents\": [\\n      \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n      \"apply\",\\n      \"check-understanding\",\\n      \"classify\",\\n      \"compare\",\\n      \"connect-back\",\\n      \"diagnose-misconception\",\\n      \"explain-cause\",\\n      \"interpret\",\\n      \"orient\",\\n      \"practise-guided\",\\n      \"practise-independent\",\\n      \"sequence\"\\n    ],\\n    \"forbidden_terminology\": [],\\n    \"required_assessment_slots\": [\\n      \"check\"\\n    ],\\n    \"rules\": {\\n      \"action_must_be_from_bound_source_allowed_actions\": true,\\n      \"copy_evidence_refs_verbatim\": true,\\n      \"copy_ids_verbatim\": true,\\n      \"item_kind_is_fixed_upstream\": true,\\n      \"multiple_choice_ids_per_block\": \"0_or_1\",\\n      \"response_action_requires_approved_source\": true,\\n      \"reuse_across_blocks\": \"forbidden\",\\n      \"selection_is_optional\": true,\\n      \"source_only_in_required_assessment_slots\": true,\\n      \"source_only_on_eligible_intent\": true,\\n      \"source_requires_learner_action\": true\\n    }\\n  },\\n  \"fixed_input\": {\\n    \"anchor\": {\\n      \"description\": \"A potted bean plant on a sunny windowsill that has grown noticeably taller over two weeks, paired with its twin kept in a dark cupboard that has stayed pale and short despite being watered the same amount.\",\\n      \"id\": \"anchor-1\"\\n    },\\n    \"approved_item_ids\": [\\n      \"910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i1\",\\n      \"910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i2\",\\n      \"910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i3\",\\n      \"910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i4\",\\n      \"910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99:9704c8b6-3b96-49d1-ade2-ad4c55329be1.i5\"\\n    ],\\n    \"lesson\": {\\n      \"grade_level\": \"Grade\",\\n      \"knowledge_type\": \"conceptual\",\\n      \"lesson_mode\": \"first_exposure\",\\n      \"objective\": \"Explain how light, water and carbon dioxide combine in the leaves to make the food a plant needs to grow, naming the process as photosynthesis.\",\\n      \"path_lesson_id\": \"910bef47-fcc3-4eca-a8a4-845c986d2d99\",\\n      \"subject\": \"Science\"\\n    },\\n    \"limits\": {\\n      \"max_blocks_per_section\": 3,\\n      \"max_sections\": 4,\\n      \"max_total_blocks\": 10\\n    },\\n    \"misconceptions\": [\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"m1\",\\n        \"statement\": \"{\\'id\\': \\'m1\\', \\'description\\': \\'The plant grows because soil provides the material for new growth, with water and light simply helping it along.\\', \\'source\\': \\'drafted\\'}\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"m2\",\\n        \"statement\": \"{\\'id\\': \\'m2\\', \\'description\\': \\'Light and carbon dioxide are only needed as comfort conditions, not as ingredients that end up inside the food the plant makes.\\', \\'source\\': \\'drafted\\'}\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"m3\",\\n        \"statement\": \"{\\'id\\': \\'m3\\', \\'description\\': \\'Oxygen is something the plant takes in rather than something it gives out as a product of making food.\\', \\'source\\': \\'drafted\\'}\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"prior_established\": [\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-1\",\\n        \"statement\": \"plants have roots, stems and leaves\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-2\",\\n        \"statement\": \"living things need food to grow\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-3\",\\n        \"statement\": \"plants produce their own food rather than absorbing it from soil\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-4\",\\n        \"statement\": \"soil supplies water and minerals, not food\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-5\",\\n        \"statement\": \"the food a plant makes is used for growth\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-6\",\\n        \"statement\": \"light is taken in by the plant and used as the energy source\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-7\",\\n        \"statement\": \"a plant kept in the dark cannot make food and will not thrive\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-8\",\\n        \"statement\": \"light energy is captured, not eaten\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-9\",\\n        \"statement\": \"water is taken in by the roots and carried to the leaves\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-10\",\\n        \"statement\": \"carbon dioxide is taken in from the air through the leaves\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-11\",\\n        \"statement\": \"these materials are changed, not simply stored\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-12\",\\n        \"statement\": \"leaves receive light and take in carbon dioxide\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-13\",\\n        \"statement\": \"water reaches the leaves from the roots\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-14\",\\n        \"statement\": \"food is made in the leaves and used or stored elsewhere\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-15\",\\n        \"statement\": \"the food made is a sugar used for growth and energy\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-16\",\\n        \"statement\": \"oxygen is produced and released\"\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"id\": \"prior-17\",\\n        \"statement\": \"the process is named photosynthesis\"\\n      }\\n    ],\\n    \"required_assessment_slots\": [\\n      \"check\"\\n    ],\\n    \"scope\": {\\n      \"must_establish\": [],\\n      \"must_not_introduce\": [],\\n      \"terminology\": []\\n    },\\n    \"slots\": [\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 2,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"Give the learner a reason to care and a concrete situation to hold.\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"orient\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": [\\n          \"orient\",\\n          \"activate-prior-knowledge\",\\n          \"emphasise\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 3,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"Establish what a good answer is judged against, before any case is seen.\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"criteria\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": []\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 3,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"Put a case and a near-miss side by side. The learner must see the boundary of the idea, not just the idea.\\\\n\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"contrast\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": [\\n          \"compare\",\\n          \"classify\",\\n          \"explain\",\\n          \"warn\",\\n          \"show-structure\"\\n        ]\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 3,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"Use the idea on a context not seen in this lesson.\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"apply\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": []\\n      },\\n      {\\n        \"max_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"min_blocks\": 1,\\n        \"purpose\": \"The shared diagnostic. Identical across every variant. Written from the card only, never from the prose above it.\\\\n\",\\n        \"slot_id\": \"check\",\\n        \"typical_intents\": [\\n          \"check-understanding\",\\n          \"practise-independent\",\\n          \"diagnose-misconception\"\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  },\\n  \"legality_catalogue_hash\": \"7ebb488b3c71bddda7dd499865b40765984ca05043a0629dbcc4be8852783baa\",\\n  \"reserved_assessment_scenarios\": [\\n    \"A s