the eventual writing deliberately: a paragraph is one idea in three or\\nfour sentences and up to 60 words; a section changes job with a subsection\\nheading (especially beyond 120 prose words); an explaining section opens with\\nexactly one key idea, ordered before any equation, compare, table or figure\\n(code reserves and places that key-idea callout slot for explaining sections, so\\ndo not spend a callout on it; use callouts for misconceptions and cautions); and a\\nspecialized form must earn its place from the block cue.\\nUse `**term**` once for key terms, `*emphasis*` for emphasis, `~subscript~`,\\n`^superscript^`, and blank lines for paragraph breaks. Chemical formulas, units\\nand exponents always use the markup (`CO~2~`, `H~2~O`, `m^2^`), never Unicode\\nsub/superscripts or bare digits like CO2. These inline tokens are\\nliteral content, not Markdown or HTML.\\nWhen the plan names a misconception for a block, prefer a `callout` with role\\n`misconception` for it where the block text carries the cue.\\n\\nParagraph runs are counted across block boundaries, not just within one block:\\nchoosing a paragraph for block A and then another paragraph for the next block\\nB continues the same run. A TaskAnchor ends a run: TaskAnchors are inserted by\\ncode after the final ordinary node of their owning block, and a block that owns\\na section task always ends the run there, so the next block starts a fresh\\ncount of zero. A block with no task does not break a run.\\n\\nBefore choosing kinds, pre-plan: scan the blocks in order and find every run of\\nthree or more consecutive blocks that would otherwise all need a paragraph (no\\nTaskAnchor breaks them). For each such run, check every block in it against the\\ncue lists above and pick an eligible non-paragraph kind (list, table, heading,\\nor callout) for at least one block in the run, using the exact cue\\nword that block's `intent`, `brief`, or `evidence` contains. If, and only if,\\nnone of the blocks in that run contain any cue for any non-paragraph kind, the\\nrun may stay all paragraphs.\\n\\nReturn only the schema fields. If `repair_errors` is non-empty, correct those\\nspecific violations while preserving every valid choice: for a paragraph-run\\nerror, the message names the blocks in the offending run, which non-paragraph\\nkind (if any) each is eligible for, and its matched cue word -- switch one of\\nthose blocks to the named kind using the named cue; for a missing-cue error,\\nswitch to a kind the message says the block is eligible for, or fall back to a\\nparagraph.\\n\"}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"$defs\": {\"CompositionChoice\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"Provider choice; identity and TaskAnchor placement remain code-owned.\", \"properties\": {\"teaching_block_id\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"kind\": {\"enum\": [\"paragraph\", \"heading\", \"list\", \"table\", \"callout\", \"equation\", \"quote\", \"compare\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"semantic_role\": {\"enum\": [\"bridge\", \"explanation\", \"worked_example\", \"interpretation\", \"summary\", \"sequence\", \"comparison\", \"evidence\", \"visual_model\", \"misconception\", \"safety_guidance\", \"subsection\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"teaching_block_id\", \"kind\", \"semantic_role\"], \"title\": \"CompositionChoice\", \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"items\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/CompositionChoice\"}, \"minItems\": 1, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"items\"], \"title\": \"SectionCompositionDraft\", \"type\": \"object\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '{\"inline_vocabulary\": {\"emphasis\": \"*text*\", \"paragraph_break\": \"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\", \"strong\": \"**text**\", \"subscript\": \"~text~\", \"superscript\": \"^text^\"}, \"repair_errors\": [], \"section\": {\"avoid_repeating\": [\"No restating of the definition of photosynthesis or of the standard.\", \"No re-use of the bean-plant pair in later sections.\"], \"blocks\": [{\"brief\": \"Name the account that the new stem and leaves were built from material supplied by the soil, with water and light only helping the plant along. State plainly what is correct instead: the soil supplies water and minerals and nothing else, so it does not supply food and does not supply the material of new growth; the plant builds that material from the sugar it makes in its leaves by combining carbon dioxide from the air with water from the roots. Show the account failing on the pair: the cupboard plant stood in the same soil and was watered exactly as the windowsill plant was, yet it built no new growth at all, so soil and water cannot be what made the difference.\", \"departure_reason\": \"The account is a held belief, not a predictable slip and not a legitimate alternative to compare, so warn and compare would both leave the wrong model in place; naming the wrong model and showing it fail on the pair is the only move that removes it.\", \"evidence\": \"The soil account is a held belief with a confident wrong answer, so it must be named and shown failing rather than merely flagged as a slip; the anchor pair\\'s identical soil and water supply the falsifying evidence inside the case the lesson already uses.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"m1\", \"prior-4\", \"anchor.anchor-1\", \"slot.contrast.purpose\"], \"id\": \"contrast-b1\", \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\", \"learner_action\": {\"action\": \"enter-text\", \"difficulty\": \"guided\", \"expected_evidence\": \"an explanation that identical soil and identical water rule the soil account out, so the difference must lie in something the windowsill plant had and the cupboard plant did not\", \"purpose\": \"make the learner produce the refutation rather than recognise it\", \"target\": \"why identical soil and identical watering cannot account for the difference between the two plants\"}, \"position\": 0, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"formative\"}, {\"brief\": \"Explain the pair\\'s outcome by the mechanism: the windowsill plant\\'s leaves captured light energy, and inside those leaves water from the roots was combined with carbon dioxide from the air to make sugar, and that sugar was used to build the taller stem, the greener leaves and the new growth. The cupboard plant had the same soil and the same water, and carbon dioxide was present in the air around it, but with no light its leaves could not combine them, so no sugar was made and no new growth was built. State plainly what is wrong with treating light and carbon dioxide as mere comfort conditions: carbon dioxide is a raw material, and its atoms become part of the sugar the plant makes; light supplies the energy that drives the combining, and that energy ends up stored in the sugar. Show the account failing: if light and carbon dioxide were only comfort conditions, the cupboard plant \\\\u2014 which had water, soil and air \\\\u2014 would have grown as well.\", \"departure_reason\": \"The contrast section needs the mechanism itself stated, not a sorting of statements: classify and compare would separate accounts without ever giving the chain that produced the growth, and the objective explicitly asks how the inputs combine.\", \"evidence\": \"The objective asks how the three inputs combine to make food, which is a mechanism linking cause to result; only a causal account of the pair can show why the same water and soil produced two different outcomes, and it is also what makes the comfort-condition account fail.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"lesson.objective\", \"anchor.anchor-1\", \"m2\", \"prior-11\", \"slot.contrast.purpose\"], \"id\": \"contrast-b2\", \"intent\": \"explain-cause\", \"learner_action\": null, \"position\": 1, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"none\"}, {\"brief\": \"Name the account that the plant takes oxygen in to make its food. State plainly what is correct instead: when water and carbon dioxide are combined into sugar in the leaves, oxygen is produced and released from the leaves into the air, so making food gives oxygen out rather than taking it in. Show the account failing: in bright light the leaves of a leafy plant give out oxygen, so the air around such a plant gains oxygen rather than losing it \\\\u2014 the opposite of what the account predicts.\", \"departure_reason\": \"This is a held belief about which way oxygen moves, not a slip to flag before it happens and not a second legitimate option to set beside the correct one, so warn and compare would leave the wrong direction intact.\", \"evidence\": \"The direction oxygen moves is a specific, confidently held error about the products of food-making, so it needs the wrong direction named and contradicted by the evidence, which a general statement that oxygen is released would not do.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"m3\", \"prior-16\", \"slot.contrast.purpose\"], \"id\": \"contrast-b3\", \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\", \"learner_action\": null, \"position\": 2, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"none\"}], \"bridge_from_previous\": \"The definition and the standard are now available, so the pair\\'s outcome can be explained causally and each competing account can be checked against the standard.\", \"display_title\": \"Explaining the two plants \\\\u2014 and the accounts that fail\", \"entry_state\": [\"Learners can state the definition of photosynthesis and the standard a complete explanation must meet.\", \"Learners hold the pair\\'s outcome and know that light was the one condition that differed.\"], \"exit_state\": [\"Learners can explain the pair\\'s growth by the mechanism and say what the cupboard plant was missing.\", \"Learners can state why the soil account, the comfort-condition account and the oxygen-intake account fail.\"], \"must_establish\": [\"The causal account of the pair: the windowsill plant\\'s leaves captured light energy and combined water with carbon dioxide to make sugar, and that sugar built the taller stem, the greener leaves and the new growth; the cupboard plant had the same soil and water and carbon dioxide in the air around it, but no light energy, so no sugar was made and no new growth was built.\", \"The correct understanding behind each failing account: soil supplies only water and minerals, never food or growth material; carbon dioxide\\'s atoms become part of the sugar and light supplies the energy that drives the combining and is stored in the sugar, so neither is a comfort condition; and oxygen is produced and released from the leaves as the sugar is made, not taken in for it.\"], \"slot_id\": \"contrast\", \"specific_purpose\": \"Explain the anchor pair by the mechanism and show the three wrong accounts failing against the standard.\", \"transition\": \"With the mechanism explained and defended on the pair, learners use it on situations the lesson has not used.\"}, \"shaping_targets\": {\"compare_items_target_max\": 3, \"equation_inputs_target_max\": 4, \"equation_outputs_target_max\": 3, \"key_idea_words_max\": 25, \"list_item_words_max\": 30, \"misconception_words_max\": {\"belief\": 30, \"conclusion\": 30, \"evidence\": 45}, \"note_words_max\": 50, \"paragraph_words_max\": 60, \"prose_section_words_target\": [150, 280], \"table_cell_words_max\": 12}, \"tasks\": [{\"action\": \"enter-text\", \"expected_evidence\": \"an explanation that identical soil and identical water rule the soil account out, so the difference must lie in something the windowsill plant had and the cupboard plant did not\", \"id\": \"task-contrast-b1\", \"purpose\": \"make the learner produce the refutation rather than recognise it\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"}]}'}], 'model': 'deepseek-flash', 'max_completion_tokens': 16000, 'stream': False}, 'extra_json': {'thinking': {'type': 'disabled'}}}"}
