`step`, `stage`,\\n  `set`, `category`, `example`, `evidence`, or `sort`.\\n- `callout` with role `explanation`, `summary`, `misconception`, or\\n  `safety_guidance`: an explaining block may carry a `key idea`; the same block\\n  text contains `key idea`, `explain`, `misconception`, `mistake`, `warning`,\\n  `safety`, or `caution`. Prefer one callout per section; the code-owned key-idea\\n  slot of an explaining section does not count (advisory, never repaired).\\n- `equation` with role `worked_example`, `explanation`, or `sequence`: the\\n  block text contains `equation`, `formula`, `input`, `output`, `process`, or\\n  `calculate`.\\n- `quote` with role `interpretation`, `evidence`, or `misconception`: the\\n  block text contains `claim`, `quote`, `says`, or `assert`.\\n- `compare` with role `comparison`: the block text contains `compare`,\\n  `contrast`, `alternative`, `option`, or `rival`.\\n\\nA `misconception` semantic role does not by itself authorize a `callout`.\\nThe same block's `intent`, `brief`, or `evidence` must contain one of the literal\\ncallout cues above. If it does not, use a `paragraph`; a paragraph may still use\\nthe `misconception` role.\\n\\nMatch roles to kinds exactly: paragraphs allow `bridge`, `explanation`,\\n`worked_example`, `interpretation`, `summary`, `evidence`, `misconception`, and\\n`safety_guidance`; headings allow only `subsection`; lists allow only `sequence`\\nand `evidence`; tables allow only `comparison` and `evidence`; callouts allow only `misconception`\\nand `safety_guidance`; equations allow `worked_example`, `explanation`, and\\n`sequence`; quotes allow `interpretation`, `evidence`, and `misconception`;\\ncompares allow only `comparison`. Keep paragraph runs to at most two, each\\nblock to at most two ordinary nodes, and the section to ten ordinary nodes as\\nwriter targets. If a target is exceeded, preserve the selected form and all\\ncontent; the validator records an advisory warning and never repairs it.\\n\\nShape the eventual writing deliberately: a paragraph is one idea in three or\\nfour sentences and up to 60 words; a section changes job with a subsection\\nheading (especially beyond 120 prose words); an explaining section opens with\\nexactly one key idea, ordered before any equation, compare, table or figure\\n(code reserves and places that key-idea callout slot for explaining sections, so\\ndo not spend a callout on it; use callouts for misconceptions and cautions); and a\\nspecialized form must earn its place from the block cue.\\nUse `**term**` once for key terms, `*emphasis*` for emphasis, `~subscript~`,\\n`^superscript^`, and blank lines for paragraph breaks. Chemical formulas, units\\nand exponents always use the markup (`CO~2~`, `H~2~O`, `m^2^`), never Unicode\\nsub/superscripts or bare digits like CO2. These inline tokens are\\nliteral content, not Markdown or HTML.\\nWhen the plan names a misconception for a block, prefer a `callout` with role\\n`misconception` for it where the block text carries the cue.\\n\\nParagraph runs are counted across block boundaries, not just within one block:\\nchoosing a paragraph for block A and then another paragraph for the next block\\nB continues the same run. A TaskAnchor ends a run: TaskAnchors are inserted by\\ncode after the final ordinary node of their owning block, and a block that owns\\na section task always ends the run there, so the next block starts a fresh\\ncount of zero. A block with no task does not break a run.\\n\\nBefore choosing kinds, pre-plan: scan the blocks in order and find every run of\\nthree or more consecutive blocks that would otherwise all need a paragraph (no\\nTaskAnchor breaks them). For each such run, check every block in it against the\\ncue lists above and pick an eligible non-paragraph kind (list, table, heading,\\nor callout) for at least one block in the run, using the exact cue\\nword that block's `intent`, `brief`, or `evidence` contains. If, and only if,\\nnone of the blocks in that run contain any cue for any non-paragraph kind, the\\nrun may stay all paragraphs.\\n\\nReturn only the schema fields. If `repair_errors` is non-empty, correct those\\nspecific violations while preserving every valid choice: for a paragraph-run\\nerror, the message names the blocks in the offending run, which non-paragraph\\nkind (if any) each is eligible for, and its matched cue word -- switch one of\\nthose blocks to the named kind using the named cue; for a missing-cue error,\\nswitch to a kind the message says the block is eligible for, or fall back to a\\nparagraph.\\n\"}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"$defs\": {\"CompositionChoice\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"Provider choice; identity and TaskAnchor placement remain code-owned.\", \"properties\": {\"teaching_block_id\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"kind\": {\"enum\": [\"paragraph\", \"heading\", \"list\", \"table\", \"callout\", \"equation\", \"quote\", \"compare\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"semantic_role\": {\"enum\": [\"bridge\", \"explanation\", \"worked_example\", \"interpretation\", \"summary\", \"sequence\", \"comparison\", \"evidence\", \"visual_model\", \"misconception\", \"safety_guidance\", \"subsection\"], \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"teaching_block_id\", \"kind\", \"semantic_role\"], \"title\": \"CompositionChoice\", \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"items\": {\"items\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/CompositionChoice\"}, \"minItems\": 1, \"type\": \"array\"}}, \"required\": [\"items\"], \"title\": \"SectionCompositionDraft\", \"type\": \"object\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '{\"inline_vocabulary\": {\"emphasis\": \"*text*\", \"paragraph_break\": \"\\\\\\\\n\\\\\\\\n\", \"strong\": \"**text**\", \"subscript\": \"~text~\", \"superscript\": \"^text^\"}, \"repair_errors\": [], \"section\": {\"avoid_repeating\": [\"No re-use of the bean-plant pair, the definition or the standard.\", \"No prompts in the second situation.\"], \"blocks\": [{\"brief\": \"Present a greenhouse of tomato vines on a bright summer morning: the grower raises the amount of carbon dioxide in the greenhouse air and the vines put on growth faster than before; on a dull, heavily overcast day the same extra carbon dioxide makes almost no difference. Prompt the learner through the reasoning without finishing it: name where the extra carbon dioxide enters the plant, name what else has to be present, and say what the leaves do with the carbon dioxide and the water when light is available. Supply the prompts, not the completed explanation.\", \"departure_reason\": \"The apply slot has no typical intents, and the learner needs a supported first attempt on an unseen situation before any unaided work, so practise-guided is the fitting permitted intent rather than a second explanation by the teacher.\", \"evidence\": \"The apply slot requires a context the lesson has not used, and this situation forces the learner to hold all three inputs together while varying only light, which the anchor pair did not ask them to state in words.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"lesson.objective\", \"slot.apply.purpose\"], \"id\": \"apply-b1\", \"intent\": \"practise-guided\", \"learner_action\": {\"action\": \"enter-text\", \"difficulty\": \"guided\", \"expected_evidence\": \"an explanation naming carbon dioxide entering the leaves, water from the roots, light energy captured in the leaves and sugar used for growth, and stating that with too little light the extra carbon dioxide cannot be combined\", \"purpose\": \"first supported use of the mechanism on a situation the lesson has not used\", \"target\": \"why added carbon dioxide speeds growth on a bright morning but not on a dull day\"}, \"position\": 0, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"formative\"}, {\"brief\": \"Present a second situation the lesson has not used: a broad-leaved houseplant in a bright room has its leaves coated with a thick layer of dust, and over the following weeks it puts on far less new growth than before, although it is watered exactly as before and the room stays just as bright. With nothing but the situation to work from, the learner explains the slowdown: less light reaches the leaves, so the leaves capture less light energy and combine less carbon dioxide and water into sugar, so less food is available for building new growth. Do not name the answer and do not restate the definition.\", \"departure_reason\": \"The apply slot has no typical intents, and after a supported attempt the learner must perform unaided; stopping at guided practice would leave the lesson without any independent use of the mechanism.\", \"evidence\": \"The learner has just succeeded with prompts, so the same mechanism must now be produced without them on a situation whose only change is the light reaching the leaves, which is the cleanest unaided test short of the check itself.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"slot.apply.purpose\", \"prior-14\", \"lesson.objective\"], \"id\": \"apply-b2\", \"intent\": \"practise-independent\", \"learner_action\": {\"action\": \"enter-text\", \"difficulty\": \"independent\", \"expected_evidence\": \"an explanation that less light reaches the leaves, so less light energy is captured, less carbon dioxide and water are combined into sugar, and less food is available for new growth\", \"purpose\": \"unaided use of the mechanism on a second unseen situation\", \"target\": \"why a dust-covered houseplant in a bright room slows its growth while its watering is unchanged\"}, \"position\": 1, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"formative\"}], \"bridge_from_previous\": \"The mechanism has been explained and defended on the bean-plant pair, so learners can now run it on cases the lesson has not used.\", \"display_title\": \"Using the idea somewhere new\", \"entry_state\": [\"Learners can explain the pair\\'s growth by the mechanism and reject the three failing accounts.\", \"Learners can name the three inputs, the leaf as the site and sugar as the product.\"], \"exit_state\": [\"Learners can explain a bright-light greenhouse and a dust-covered houseplant by the inputs, the leaf site, the sugar produced and the light energy that drives the combining.\", \"Learners can produce the second explanation unaided.\"], \"must_establish\": [\"That extra carbon dioxide speeds a greenhouse crop only when there is enough light for the leaves to combine it with water into sugar.\", \"That anything reducing the light reaching the leaves reduces the sugar made and therefore the new growth, even when the water and the carbon dioxide are unchanged.\"], \"slot_id\": \"apply\", \"specific_purpose\": \"Run the mechanism on two situations the lesson has not used, first with prompts and then unaided.\", \"transition\": \"The final step measures whether that explanation is in place with no support at all.\"}, \"shaping_targets\": {\"compare_items_target_max\": 3, \"equation_inputs_target_max\": 4, \"equation_outputs_target_max\": 3, \"key_idea_words_max\": 25, \"list_item_words_max\": 30, \"misconception_words_max\": {\"belief\": 30, \"conclusion\": 30, \"evidence\": 45}, \"note_words_max\": 50, \"paragraph_words_max\": 60, \"prose_section_words_target\": [150, 280], \"table_cell_words_max\": 12}, \"tasks\": [{\"action\": \"enter-text\", \"expected_evidence\": \"an explanation naming carbon dioxide entering the leaves, water from the roots, light energy captured in the leaves and sugar used for growth, and stating that with too little light the extra carbon dioxide cannot be combined\", \"id\": \"task-apply-b1\", \"purpose\": \"first supported use of the mechanism on a situation the lesson has not used\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"apply-b1\"}, {\"action\": \"enter-text\", \"expected_evidence\": \"an explanation that less light reaches the leaves, so less light energy is captured, less carbon dioxide and water are combined into sugar, and less food is available for new growth\", \"id\": \"task-apply-b2\", \"purpose\": \"unaided use of the mechanism on a second unseen situation\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"apply-b2\"}]}'}], 'model': 'deepseek-flash', 'max_completion_tokens': 16000, 'stream': False}, 'extra_json': {'thinking': {'type': 'disabled'}}}"}
