{"timestamp": "2026-10-05T20:30:47.322360+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-794e7077-ac4a-4fe2-8484-efb4db83908b', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': '# Shared Section Boundary Validator\\n\\nReview the boundary between two already accepted shared lesson sections.\\nUse only the supplied previous section exit state and final nodes, and the\\nnext section entry state, bridge, and first nodes. Return exactly one of:\\n\\n```json\\n{\"status\":\"pass\"}\\n```\\n\\nor a typed issue:\\n\\n```json\\n{\"status\":\"issue\",\"issue\":{\"issue_code\":\"...\",\"affected_section_id\":\"...\",\"affected_node_ids\":[],\"explanation\":\"...\",\"required_correction\":\"...\"}}\\n```\\n\\nThe issue must target the previous or next section in the supplied boundary.\\nDescribe the continuity defect and the smallest learner-facing correction.\\nJudge meaning, not word overlap: paraphrases of an approved bridge, entry\\nstate, or exit state count as covered when the supplied nodes clearly express\\nthe same idea. Return an issue only when the needed idea is genuinely missing,\\ncontradicted, or too weak to support the next section.\\nDo not write replacement section content. Do not mention internal IDs,\\ncomposition plans, renderer paths, Learn, Print, model planning, or this\\nreview process in the issue text.\\n'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"$defs\": {\"ContinuityIssue\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"A typed, actionable QA result. Validators never rewrite content.\", \"properties\": {\"issue_code\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"affected_section_id\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"affected_node_ids\": {\"default\": [], \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"explanation\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"required_correction\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"issue_code\", \"affected_section_id\", \"explanation\", \"required_correction\"], \"title\": \"ContinuityIssue\", \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"status\": {\"enum\": [\"pass\", \"issue\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"issue\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/ContinuityIssue\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}}, \"required\": [\"status\"], \"title\": \"BoundarySemanticVerdict\", \"type\": \"object\", \"description\": \"Closed reviewer output: exactly PASS or one typed continuity issue.\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '{\"next_bridge\": \"The mechanism has been explained and defended on the bean-plant pair, so learners can now run it on cases the lesson has not used.\", \"next_entry_state\": [\"Learners can explain the pair\\'s growth by the mechanism and reject the three failing accounts.\", \"Learners can name the three inputs, the leaf as the site and sugar as the product.\"], \"next_first_nodes\": [{\"accessibility\": {\"description\": \"\"}, \"display\": {\"text\": \"**You have already worked out how the inputs become sugar and growth; now use that reasoning in two new situations.** The first situation gives you prompts to guide your thinking. The second asks you to produce the explanation without prompts.\"}, \"id\": \"shared-node-b8284177470216edc90b\", \"kind\": \"paragraph\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"apply-b1\"}, {\"accessibility\": {\"description\": \"\"}, \"display\": {\"text\": \"Use the same reasoning you built earlier. Trace each input from where it enters the plant to where it is used in the leaves. Consider what else the leaves need in order to use the carbon dioxide and water. Then connect that leaf action to the growth you observe.\"}, \"id\": \"shared-node-8cd71a77b870ea8ae553\", \"kind\": \"paragraph\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"apply-b1\"}, {\"id\": \"shared-task-anchor-04f09b3df21b782567ac\", \"kind\": \"task_anchor\", \"role\": null, \"task_spec_id\": \"task-apply-b1\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"apply-b1\"}], \"next_plan\": {\"avoid_repeating\": [\"No re-use of the bean-plant pair, the definition or the standard.\", \"No prompts in the second situation.\"], \"blocks\": [{\"brief\": \"Present a greenhouse of tomato vines on a bright summer morning: the grower raises the amount of carbon dioxide in the greenhouse air and the vines put on growth faster than before; on a dull, heavily overcast day the same extra carbon dioxide makes almost no difference. Prompt the learner through the reasoning without finishing it: name where the extra carbon dioxide enters the plant, name what else has to be present, and say what the leaves do with the carbon dioxide and the water when light is available. Supply the prompts, not the completed explanation.\", \"departure_reason\": \"The apply slot has no typical intents, and the learner needs a supported first attempt on an unseen situation before any unaided work, so practise-guided is the fitting permitted intent rather than a second explanation by the teacher.\", \"evidence\": \"The apply slot requires a context the lesson has not used, and this situation forces the learner to hold all three inputs together while varying only light, which the anchor pair did not ask them to state in words.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"lesson.objective\", \"slot.apply.purpose\"], \"id\": \"apply-b1\", \"intent\": \"practise-guided\", \"learner_action\": {\"action\": \"enter-text\", \"difficulty\": \"guided\", \"expected_evidence\": \"an explanation naming carbon dioxide entering the leaves, water from the roots, light energy captured in the leaves and sugar used for growth, and stating that with too little light the extra carbon dioxide cannot be combined\", \"purpose\": \"first supported use of the mechanism on a situation the lesson has not used\", \"target\": \"why added carbon dioxide speeds growth on a bright morning but not on a dull day\"}, \"position\": 0, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"formative\"}, {\"brief\": \"Present a second situation the lesson has not used: a broad-leaved houseplant in a bright room has its leaves coated with a thick layer of dust, and over the following weeks it puts on far less new growth than before, although it is watered exactly as before and the room stays just as bright. With nothing but the situation to work from, the learner explains the slowdown: less light reaches the leaves, so the leaves capture less light energy and combine less carbon dioxide and water into sugar, so less food is available for building new growth. Do not name the answer and do not restate the definition.\", \"departure_reason\": \"The apply slot has no typical intents, and after a supported attempt the learner must perform unaided; stopping at guided practice would leave the lesson without any independent use of the mechanism.\", \"evidence\": \"The learner has just succeeded with prompts, so the same mechanism must now be produced without them on a situation whose only change is the light reaching the leaves, which is the cleanest unaided test short of the check itself.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"slot.apply.purpose\", \"prior-14\", \"lesson.objective\"], \"id\": \"apply-b2\", \"intent\": \"practise-independent\", \"learner_action\": {\"action\": \"enter-text\", \"difficulty\": \"independent\", \"expected_evidence\": \"an explanation that less light reaches the leaves, so less light energy is captured, less carbon dioxide and water are combined into sugar, and less food is available for new growth\", \"purpose\": \"unaided use of the mechanism on a second unseen situation\", \"target\": \"why a dust-covered houseplant in a bright room slows its growth while its watering is unchanged\"}, \"position\": 1, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"formative\"}], \"bridge_from_previous\": \"The mechanism has been explained and defended on the bean-plant pair, so learners can now run it on cases the lesson has not used.\", \"display_title\": \"Using the idea somewhere new\", \"entry_state\": [\"Learners can explain the pair\\'s growth by the mechanism and reject the three failing accounts.\", \"Learners can name the three inputs, the leaf as the site and sugar as the product.\"], \"exit_state\": [\"Learners can explain a bright-light greenhouse and a dust-covered houseplant by the inputs, the leaf site, the sugar produced and the light energy that drives the combining.\", \"Learners can produce the second explanation unaided.\"], \"must_establish\": [\"That extra carbon dioxide speeds a greenhouse crop only when there is enough light for the leaves to combine it with water into sugar.\", \"That anything reducing the light reaching the leaves reduces the sugar made and therefore the new growth, even when the water and the carbon dioxide are unchanged.\"], \"slot_id\": \"apply\", \"specific_purpose\": \"Run the mechanism on two situations the lesson has not used, first with prompts and then unaided.\", \"transition\": \"The final step measures whether that explanation is in place with no support at all.\"}, \"previous_exit_state\": [\"Learners can explain the pair\\'s growth by the mechanism and say what the cupboard plant was missing.\", \"Learners can state why the soil account, the comfort-condition account and the oxygen-intake account fail.\"], \"previous_final_nodes\": [{\"id\": \"shared-task-anchor-be51521b23581d0c80da\", \"kind\": \"task_anchor\", \"role\": null, \"task_spec_id\": \"task-contrast-b1\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"}, {\"accessibility\": {\"description\": \"Explanation: the windowsill plant\\'s leaves made sugar by combining water from the roots with carbon dioxide from the air using captured light energy, and that sugar built the new growth; the cupboard plant, with the same soil and water and with carbon dioxide around it, made no sugar without light. Carbon dioxide is a raw material and light supplies stored energy, so neither is merely a comfort condition.\"}, \"display\": {\"text\": \"The windowsill plant\\'s leaves captured light energy. Inside those leaves, water drawn from the roots was combined with carbon dioxide from the air, and the result was sugar. That sugar was then used to build the taller stem, the greener leaves and the new growth that appeared.\\\\n\\\\nThe cupboard plant had the same soil and the same water, and carbon dioxide was present in the air around it. Without light, its leaves could not combine water and carbon dioxide, so no sugar was made and no new growth was built.\\\\n\\\\nCarbon dioxide is not a comfort condition: its atoms become part of the sugar. Light is not one either: it supplies the energy that drives the combining, and that energy is stored in the sugar. If light and carbon dioxide were only comfort conditions, the cupboard plant, which had soil, water and air, would have grown as well.\"}, \"id\": \"shared-node-dcfaa39ede0c1e623c54\", \"kind\": \"paragraph\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b2\"}, {\"accessibility\": {\"description\": \"Misconception callout: the belief that plants take oxygen in to make food, tested by leaves giving out oxygen in bright light, and corrected: making sugar in the leaves produces and releases oxygen rather than consuming it.\"}, \"display\": {\"belief\": \"The plant breathes in oxygen through its leaves and uses that oxygen to make its food.\", \"conclusion\": \"When water and carbon dioxide are combined into sugar in the leaves, oxygen is produced and released into the air. Making food gives oxygen out, not in.\", \"evidence\": \"In bright light, the leaves of a leafy plant give out oxygen. The air around such a plant therefore gains oxygen rather than losing it, which is the opposite of what the account predicts.\", \"title\": \"The oxygen-intake account\", \"tone\": \"warning\", \"variant\": \"misconception\"}, \"id\": \"shared-node-ff6c81d8bad6f602a745\", \"kind\": \"callout\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b3\"}], \"previous_plan\": {\"avoid_repeating\": [\"No restating of the definition of photosynthesis or of the standard.\", \"No re-use of the bean-plant pair in later sections.\"], \"blocks\": [{\"brief\": \"Name the account that the new stem and leaves were built from material supplied by the soil, with water and light only helping the plant along. State plainly what is correct instead: the soil supplies water and minerals and nothing else, so it does not supply food and does not supply the material of new growth; the plant builds that material from the sugar it makes in its leaves by combining carbon dioxide from the air with water from the roots. Show the account failing on the pair: the cupboard plant stood in the same soil and was watered exactly as the windowsill plant was, yet it built no new growth at all, so soil and water cannot be what made the difference.\", \"departure_reason\": \"The account is a held belief, not a predictable slip and not a legitimate alternative to compare, so warn and compare would both leave the wrong model in place; naming the wrong model and showing it fail on the pair is the only move that removes it.\", \"evidence\": \"The soil account is a held belief with a confident wrong answer, so it must be named and shown failing rather than merely flagged as a slip; the anchor pair\\'s identical soil and water supply the falsifying evidence inside the case the lesson already uses.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"m1\", \"prior-4\", \"anchor.anchor-1\", \"slot.contrast.purpose\"], \"id\": \"contrast-b1\", \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\", \"learner_action\": {\"action\": \"enter-text\", \"difficulty\": \"guided\", \"expected_evidence\": \"an explanation that identical soil and identical water rule the soil account out, so the difference must lie in something the windowsill plant had and the cupboard plant did not\", \"purpose\": \"make the learner produce the refutation rather than recognise it\", \"target\": \"why identical soil and identical watering cannot account for the difference between the two plants\"}, \"position\": 0, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"formative\"}, {\"brief\": \"Explain the pair\\'s outcome by the mechanism: the windowsill plant\\'s leaves captured light energy, and inside those leaves water from the roots was combined with carbon dioxide from the air to make sugar, and that sugar was used to build the taller stem, the greener leaves and the new growth. The cupboard plant had the same soil and the same water, and carbon dioxide was present in the air around it, but with no light its leaves could not combine them, so no sugar was made and no new growth was built. State plainly what is wrong with treating light and carbon dioxide as mere comfort conditions: carbon dioxide is a raw material, and its atoms become part of the sugar the plant makes; light supplies the energy that drives the combining, and that energy ends up stored in the sugar. Show the account failing: if light and carbon dioxide were only comfort conditions, the cupboard plant \\\\u2014 which had water, soil and air \\\\u2014 would have grown as well.\", \"departure_reason\": \"The contrast section needs the mechanism itself stated, not a sorting of statements: classify and compare would separate accounts without ever giving the chain that produced the growth, and the objective explicitly asks how the inputs combine.\", \"evidence\": \"The objective asks how the three inputs combine to make food, which is a mechanism linking cause to result; only a causal account of the pair can show why the same water and soil produced two different outcomes, and it is also what makes the comfort-condition account fail.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"lesson.objective\", \"anchor.anchor-1\", \"m2\", \"prior-11\", \"slot.contrast.purpose\"], \"id\": \"contrast-b2\", \"intent\": \"explain-cause\", \"learner_action\": null, \"position\": 1, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"none\"}, {\"brief\": \"Name the account that the plant takes oxygen in to make its food. State plainly what is correct instead: when water and carbon dioxide are combined into sugar in the leaves, oxygen is produced and released from the leaves into the air, so making food gives oxygen out rather than taking it in. Show the account failing: in bright light the leaves of a leafy plant give out oxygen, so the air around such a plant gains oxygen rather than losing it \\\\u2014 the opposite of what the account predicts.\", \"departure_reason\": \"This is a held belief about which way oxygen moves, not a slip to flag before it happens and not a second legitimate option to set beside the correct one, so warn and compare would leave the wrong direction intact.\", \"evidence\": \"The direction oxygen moves is a specific, confidently held error about the products of food-making, so it needs the wrong direction named and contradicted by the evidence, which a general statement that oxygen is released would not do.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"m3\", \"prior-16\", \"slot.contrast.purpose\"], \"id\": \"contrast-b3\", \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\", \"learner_action\": null, \"position\": 2, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"none\"}], \"bridge_from_previous\": \"The definition and the standard are now available, so the pair\\'s outcome can be explained causally and each competing account can be checked against the standard.\", \"display_title\": \"Explaining the two plants \\\\u2014 and the accounts that fail\", \"entry_state\": [\"Learners can state the definition of photosynthesis and the standard a complete explanation must meet.\", \"Learners hold the pair\\'s outcome and know that light was the one condition that differed.\"], \"exit_state\": [\"Learners can explain the pair\\'s growth by the mechanism and say what the cupboard plant was missing.\", \"Learners can state why the soil account, the comfort-condition account and the oxygen-intake account fail.\"], \"must_establish\": [\"The causal account of the pair: the windowsill plant\\'s leaves captured light energy and combined water with carbon dioxide to make sugar, and that sugar built the taller stem, the greener leaves and the new growth; the cupboard plant had the same soil and water and carbon dioxide in the air around it, but no light energy, so no sugar was made and no new growth was built.\", \"The correct understanding behind each failing account: soil supplies only water and minerals, never food or growth material; carbon dioxide\\'s atoms become part of the sugar and light supplies the energy that drives the combining and is stored in the sugar, so neither is a comfort condition; and oxygen is produced and released from the leaves as the sugar is made, not taken in for it.\"], \"slot_id\": \"contrast\", \"specific_purpose\": \"Explain the anchor pair by the mechanism and show the three wrong accounts failing against the standard.\", \"transition\": \"With the mechanism explained and defended on the pair, learners use it on situations the lesson has not used.\"}}'}], 'model': 'deepseek-flash', 'max_completion_tokens': 16000, 'stream': False}, 'extra_json': {'thinking': {'type': 'disabled'}}}"}
