{"timestamp": "2026-10-05T20:30:47.429271+00:00", "level": "DEBUG", "logger": "openai._base_client", "message": "Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'User-Agent': 'pydantic-ai/1.107.1'}, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-f25d1297-37c6-4e27-a2eb-c8e3a63b0751', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': '# Shared Section Boundary Validator\\n\\nReview the boundary between two already accepted shared lesson sections.\\nUse only the supplied previous section exit state and final nodes, and the\\nnext section entry state, bridge, and first nodes. Return exactly one of:\\n\\n```json\\n{\"status\":\"pass\"}\\n```\\n\\nor a typed issue:\\n\\n```json\\n{\"status\":\"issue\",\"issue\":{\"issue_code\":\"...\",\"affected_section_id\":\"...\",\"affected_node_ids\":[],\"explanation\":\"...\",\"required_correction\":\"...\"}}\\n```\\n\\nThe issue must target the previous or next section in the supplied boundary.\\nDescribe the continuity defect and the smallest learner-facing correction.\\nJudge meaning, not word overlap: paraphrases of an approved bridge, entry\\nstate, or exit state count as covered when the supplied nodes clearly express\\nthe same idea. Return an issue only when the needed idea is genuinely missing,\\ncontradicted, or too weak to support the next section.\\nDo not write replacement section content. Do not mention internal IDs,\\ncomposition plans, renderer paths, Learn, Print, model planning, or this\\nreview process in the issue text.\\n'}, {'role': 'system', 'content': 'Return only valid JSON matching this schema.\\nDo not call tools.\\n{\"$defs\": {\"ContinuityIssue\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"A typed, actionable QA result. Validators never rewrite content.\", \"properties\": {\"issue_code\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"affected_section_id\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"affected_node_ids\": {\"default\": [], \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, \"type\": \"array\"}, \"explanation\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}, \"required_correction\": {\"minLength\": 1, \"type\": \"string\"}}, \"required\": [\"issue_code\", \"affected_section_id\", \"explanation\", \"required_correction\"], \"title\": \"ContinuityIssue\", \"type\": \"object\"}}, \"additionalProperties\": false, \"properties\": {\"status\": {\"enum\": [\"pass\", \"issue\"], \"type\": \"string\"}, \"issue\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/ContinuityIssue\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}}, \"required\": [\"status\"], \"title\": \"BoundarySemanticVerdict\", \"type\": \"object\", \"description\": \"Closed reviewer output: exactly PASS or one typed continuity issue.\"}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '{\"next_bridge\": \"The definition and the standard are now available, so the pair\\'s outcome can be explained causally and each competing account can be checked against the standard.\", \"next_entry_state\": [\"Learners can state the definition of photosynthesis and the standard a complete explanation must meet.\", \"Learners hold the pair\\'s outcome and know that light was the one condition that differed.\"], \"next_first_nodes\": [{\"accessibility\": {\"description\": \"Key idea: soil supplies water and minerals only, and new growth is built from sugar made in the leaves from carbon dioxide and water using light energy.\"}, \"display\": {\"body\": \"**Soil supplies water and minerals only; the plant builds new growth from sugar made in its leaves, using carbon dioxide, water and light energy.**\", \"title\": \"Where new growth comes from\", \"tone\": \"important\", \"variant\": \"key_idea\"}, \"id\": \"shared-key-idea-dc0d5a021ecf27ece901\", \"kind\": \"callout\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"}, {\"accessibility\": {\"description\": \"Misconception callout: the belief that the soil supplied the material of the new stem and leaves, tested by the pair\\'s identical soil and watering, and corrected: soil supplies water and minerals only, never food or growth material.\"}, \"display\": {\"belief\": \"The new stem and leaves were built from material the soil supplied; water and light only helped the plant along.\", \"conclusion\": \"Soil supplies water and minerals and nothing else. It does not supply food, and it does not supply the material of new growth.\", \"evidence\": \"Both plants stood in the same soil and were watered exactly alike, yet only the windowsill plant produced new growth. If soil material built the new growth, the cupboard plant would have grown too.\", \"title\": \"The soil account\", \"tone\": \"warning\", \"variant\": \"misconception\"}, \"id\": \"shared-node-9eef5f7e8590911e8a8b\", \"kind\": \"callout\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"}, {\"id\": \"shared-task-anchor-be51521b23581d0c80da\", \"kind\": \"task_anchor\", \"role\": null, \"task_spec_id\": \"task-contrast-b1\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"contrast-b1\"}], \"next_plan\": {\"avoid_repeating\": [\"No restating of the definition of photosynthesis or of the standard.\", \"No re-use of the bean-plant pair in later sections.\"], \"blocks\": [{\"brief\": \"Name the account that the new stem and leaves were built from material supplied by the soil, with water and light only helping the plant along. State plainly what is correct instead: the soil supplies water and minerals and nothing else, so it does not supply food and does not supply the material of new growth; the plant builds that material from the sugar it makes in its leaves by combining carbon dioxide from the air with water from the roots. Show the account failing on the pair: the cupboard plant stood in the same soil and was watered exactly as the windowsill plant was, yet it built no new growth at all, so soil and water cannot be what made the difference.\", \"departure_reason\": \"The account is a held belief, not a predictable slip and not a legitimate alternative to compare, so warn and compare would both leave the wrong model in place; naming the wrong model and showing it fail on the pair is the only move that removes it.\", \"evidence\": \"The soil account is a held belief with a confident wrong answer, so it must be named and shown failing rather than merely flagged as a slip; the anchor pair\\'s identical soil and water supply the falsifying evidence inside the case the lesson already uses.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"m1\", \"prior-4\", \"anchor.anchor-1\", \"slot.contrast.purpose\"], \"id\": \"contrast-b1\", \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\", \"learner_action\": {\"action\": \"enter-text\", \"difficulty\": \"guided\", \"expected_evidence\": \"an explanation that identical soil and identical water rule the soil account out, so the difference must lie in something the windowsill plant had and the cupboard plant did not\", \"purpose\": \"make the learner produce the refutation rather than recognise it\", \"target\": \"why identical soil and identical watering cannot account for the difference between the two plants\"}, \"position\": 0, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"formative\"}, {\"brief\": \"Explain the pair\\'s outcome by the mechanism: the windowsill plant\\'s leaves captured light energy, and inside those leaves water from the roots was combined with carbon dioxide from the air to make sugar, and that sugar was used to build the taller stem, the greener leaves and the new growth. The cupboard plant had the same soil and the same water, and carbon dioxide was present in the air around it, but with no light its leaves could not combine them, so no sugar was made and no new growth was built. State plainly what is wrong with treating light and carbon dioxide as mere comfort conditions: carbon dioxide is a raw material, and its atoms become part of the sugar the plant makes; light supplies the energy that drives the combining, and that energy ends up stored in the sugar. Show the account failing: if light and carbon dioxide were only comfort conditions, the cupboard plant \\\\u2014 which had water, soil and air \\\\u2014 would have grown as well.\", \"departure_reason\": \"The contrast section needs the mechanism itself stated, not a sorting of statements: classify and compare would separate accounts without ever giving the chain that produced the growth, and the objective explicitly asks how the inputs combine.\", \"evidence\": \"The objective asks how the three inputs combine to make food, which is a mechanism linking cause to result; only a causal account of the pair can show why the same water and soil produced two different outcomes, and it is also what makes the comfort-condition account fail.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"lesson.objective\", \"anchor.anchor-1\", \"m2\", \"prior-11\", \"slot.contrast.purpose\"], \"id\": \"contrast-b2\", \"intent\": \"explain-cause\", \"learner_action\": null, \"position\": 1, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"none\"}, {\"brief\": \"Name the account that the plant takes oxygen in to make its food. State plainly what is correct instead: when water and carbon dioxide are combined into sugar in the leaves, oxygen is produced and released from the leaves into the air, so making food gives oxygen out rather than taking it in. Show the account failing: in bright light the leaves of a leafy plant give out oxygen, so the air around such a plant gains oxygen rather than losing it \\\\u2014 the opposite of what the account predicts.\", \"departure_reason\": \"This is a held belief about which way oxygen moves, not a slip to flag before it happens and not a second legitimate option to set beside the correct one, so warn and compare would leave the wrong direction intact.\", \"evidence\": \"The direction oxygen moves is a specific, confidently held error about the products of food-making, so it needs the wrong direction named and contradicted by the evidence, which a general statement that oxygen is released would not do.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"m3\", \"prior-16\", \"slot.contrast.purpose\"], \"id\": \"contrast-b3\", \"intent\": \"diagnose-misconception\", \"learner_action\": null, \"position\": 2, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"none\"}], \"bridge_from_previous\": \"The definition and the standard are now available, so the pair\\'s outcome can be explained causally and each competing account can be checked against the standard.\", \"display_title\": \"Explaining the two plants \\\\u2014 and the accounts that fail\", \"entry_state\": [\"Learners can state the definition of photosynthesis and the standard a complete explanation must meet.\", \"Learners hold the pair\\'s outcome and know that light was the one condition that differed.\"], \"exit_state\": [\"Learners can explain the pair\\'s growth by the mechanism and say what the cupboard plant was missing.\", \"Learners can state why the soil account, the comfort-condition account and the oxygen-intake account fail.\"], \"must_establish\": [\"The causal account of the pair: the windowsill plant\\'s leaves captured light energy and combined water with carbon dioxide to make sugar, and that sugar built the taller stem, the greener leaves and the new growth; the cupboard plant had the same soil and water and carbon dioxide in the air around it, but no light energy, so no sugar was made and no new growth was built.\", \"The correct understanding behind each failing account: soil supplies only water and minerals, never food or growth material; carbon dioxide\\'s atoms become part of the sugar and light supplies the energy that drives the combining and is stored in the sugar, so neither is a comfort condition; and oxygen is produced and released from the leaves as the sugar is made, not taken in for it.\"], \"slot_id\": \"contrast\", \"specific_purpose\": \"Explain the anchor pair by the mechanism and show the three wrong accounts failing against the standard.\", \"transition\": \"With the mechanism explained and defended on the pair, learners use it on situations the lesson has not used.\"}, \"previous_exit_state\": [\"Learners can state what photosynthesis is, name the three inputs and the leaf as the site, and name the product as food used for growth.\", \"Learners can say what a complete explanation must include and what it must not credit.\"], \"previous_final_nodes\": [{\"accessibility\": {\"description\": \"Key idea: photosynthesis combines water and carbon dioxide in leaves using light energy to make sugar and release oxygen.\"}, \"display\": {\"body\": \"**Photosynthesis is the process in which leaves use light energy to combine water and carbon dioxide into sugar, the plant\\'s food, releasing oxygen.**\", \"title\": \"\", \"tone\": \"note\", \"variant\": \"key_idea\"}, \"id\": \"shared-key-idea-7fe844430e59f5140b20\", \"kind\": \"callout\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"criteria-b1\"}, {\"accessibility\": {\"description\": \"Defines photosynthesis, names the three inputs and the leaf as the site, names the product as food used for growth, sets the standard for a complete explanation, and corrects the idea that soil supplies food or the material of new growth.\"}, \"display\": {\"text\": \"Photosynthesis is the process in which light energy captured in the leaves combines water with carbon dioxide to make sugar, the plant\\'s food, and to release oxygen. The water is taken in by the roots and carried up the stem; carbon dioxide enters the leaves from the air. This sugar is the plant\\'s food, used for growth and as an energy source.\\\\n\\\\nA complete explanation of a plant\\'s growth must meet a clear standard. It must name light, water and carbon dioxide as the three inputs, place the combining in the leaves, name the product as food, a sugar, used for growth, and name the process photosynthesis.\\\\n\\\\nAn explanation that credits the soil with supplying food, or with supplying the material of new growth, does not meet the standard. The soil supplies water and dissolved minerals; the sugar the plant uses for growth is made inside the leaf, from water and carbon dioxide, using light energy.\\\\n\\\\nThe bean-plant case from the opening is still waiting to be explained.\"}, \"id\": \"shared-node-93018d86849e35fab26d\", \"kind\": \"paragraph\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"criteria-b1\"}, {\"accessibility\": {\"alt_text\": \"\"}, \"display\": {\"asset_id\": null, \"caption\": \"The diagram shows light reaching the leaf from the Sun, carbon dioxide in the air entering the leaf, and water travelling from the roots up the stem into the leaf. It also shows water and carbon dioxide combining inside the leaf, driven by light energy, to make sugar, the plant\\'s food, sugar leaving the leaf to be used for growth and stored elsewhere in the plant, and oxygen being released from the leaf into the air as the sugar is made.\"}, \"id\": \"shared-figure-node-7fe844430e59f5140b20\", \"kind\": \"figure\", \"teaching_block_id\": \"criteria-b1\"}], \"previous_plan\": {\"avoid_repeating\": [\"No re-description of the bean-plant pair beyond naming it as the case still waiting to be explained.\", \"No judgement yet about which accounts of growth fail.\"], \"blocks\": [{\"brief\": \"Give the precise definition the lesson will use: photosynthesis is the process in which light energy captured in the leaves combines water \\\\u2014 taken in by the roots and carried up the stem \\\\u2014 with carbon dioxide taken in from the air through the leaves, to make sugar, the plant\\'s food, and to release oxygen; that sugar is what the plant uses for growth and as an energy source. Then set the standard a complete explanation of a plant\\'s growth must meet: light, water and carbon dioxide named as the three inputs; the combining placed in the leaves; the product named as food, a sugar, used for growth; the process named photosynthesis. State that an explanation which credits the soil with supplying food or with supplying the material of new growth does not meet the standard. Do not explain the bean-plant pair yet.\", \"departure_reason\": \"The criteria slot supplies no typical intents, so the slot\\'s own job \\\\u2014 establishing the definition and the standard \\\\u2014 has to be carried by a permitted intent that fixes meaning precisely, and define is the only permitted intent written for that.\", \"evidence\": \"The objective requires the learner to explain the process and name it, so the lesson must fix the definition and the acceptance standard before any case is judged, which is exactly what this slot places before a case is seen.\", \"evidence_refs\": [\"lesson.objective\", \"prior-17\", \"prior-4\", \"prior-16\", \"slot.criteria.purpose\"], \"id\": \"criteria-b1\", \"intent\": \"define\", \"learner_action\": null, \"position\": 0, \"source_question_ids\": [], \"sourcebook_needs\": [], \"sourcebook_refs\": [], \"stimulus_dependencies\": [], \"task_mode\": \"none\", \"visual\": {\"labels_required\": [\"light\", \"carbon dioxide\", \"water\", \"roots\", \"stem\", \"leaf\", \"sugar\", \"food\", \"oxygen\"], \"mode\": \"diagram\", \"must_not_show\": [\"soil supplying food or the material of new growth\", \"the plant taking oxygen in for food-making\", \"chemical formulas or equations\"], \"must_show\": [\"light reaching the leaf from the Sun\", \"carbon dioxide in the air entering the leaf\", \"water travelling from the roots up the stem into the leaf\", \"water and carbon dioxide combining inside the leaf, driven by light energy, to make sugar, the plant\\'s food\", \"sugar leaving the leaf to be used for growth and stored elsewhere in the plant\", \"oxygen being released from the leaf into the air as the sugar is made\"], \"purpose\": \"Show how light, water and carbon dioxide come together inside a leaf to make the sugar the plant uses for growth, and that oxygen leaves the leaf as this happens.\", \"required\": true}}], \"bridge_from_previous\": \"The opening left the pair\\'s growth unexplained and confirmed where each input enters the plant, so the lesson can now state the process those inputs take part in and the standard the explanation must meet.\", \"display_title\": \"What photosynthesis is, and what a full answer must contain\", \"entry_state\": [\"Learners hold the bean-plant difference as an open question.\", \"Learners can name the route of water, carbon dioxide and light into the plant.\"], \"exit_state\": [\"Learners can state what photosynthesis is, name the three inputs and the leaf as the site, and name the product as food used for growth.\", \"Learners can say what a complete explanation must include and what it must not credit.\"], \"must_establish\": [\"The definition of photosynthesis: light energy captured in the leaves combines water from the roots with carbon dioxide from the air to make sugar, the plant\\'s food, and to release oxygen; the sugar is what the plant uses for growth and as an energy source.\", \"The standard a complete explanation of a plant\\'s growth must meet: light, water and carbon dioxide named as the three inputs; the combining placed in the leaves; the product named as food, a sugar, used for growth; the process named photosynthesis; and no credit given to the soil for supplying food or the material of new growth.\"], \"slot_id\": \"criteria\", \"specific_purpose\": \"Define photosynthesis precisely and fix the standard a complete explanation must meet, before the anchor pair is explained.\", \"transition\": \"With the definition and the standard fixed, the lesson returns to the bean-plant pair to explain it and to test the accounts that fail.\"}}'}], 'model': 'deepseek-flash', 'max_completion_tokens': 16000, 'stream': False}, 'extra_json': {'thinking': {'type': 'disabled'}}}"}
